WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.655
Input validation is a core reliability,
maintainability,

2
00:00:03.735 --> 00:00:07.775
and security practice in C because C
programs often operate close to memory,

3
00:00:07.855 --> 00:00:11.641
buffers, files, device interfaces, and
operating-system resources.

4
00:00:11.721 --> 00:00:15.381
A value that looks like ordinary data can
influence allocation size,

5
00:00:15.461 --> 00:00:20.005
array indexing, file selection, parsing
decisions, control flow, or state changes.

6
00:00:20.085 --> 00:00:23.493
Invalid input usually causes trouble by
breaking assumptions.

7
00:00:23.573 --> 00:00:27.802
The code may assume a string is
terminated, a number fits a range, a field exists,

8
00:00:27.882 --> 00:00:30.532
a delimiter appears once, a byte sequence
is text,

9
00:00:30.612 --> 00:00:32.947
or a length value matches the actual
buffer.

10
00:00:33.027 --> 00:00:35.173
When those assumptions are not checked,

11
00:00:35.253 --> 00:00:37.904
later code may make decisions on a false
contract.

12
00:00:37.984 --> 00:00:41.897
Validation means making expectations
explicit before input is trusted.

13
00:00:41.977 --> 00:00:45.637
The program should define the source,
expected type, range, length,

14
00:00:45.717 --> 00:00:48.178
format, encoding, and state rules for a
value.

15
00:00:48.258 --> 00:00:52.423
Only after the value satisfies those rules
should it influence memory access,

16
00:00:52.503 --> 00:00:55.406
resource use, policy decisions, or
persistent state.

17
00:00:55.486 --> 00:00:58.516
Validation is not a complete security
program by itself.

18
00:00:58.596 --> 00:01:02.445
It works together with memory-safe coding
practices, careful parsing,

19
00:01:02.525 --> 00:01:06.754
clear error handling, output encoding
where relevant, authorization checks,

20
00:01:06.834 --> 00:01:09.358
logging discipline, testing, and code
review.

21
00:01:09.438 --> 00:01:13.730
The goal is to keep invalid or ambiguous
data from reaching code that assumes it is

22
00:01:13.810 --> 00:01:14.567
already safe.

23
00:01:14.647 --> 00:01:17.045
Good validation also improves maintenance.

24
00:01:17.125 --> 00:01:20.912
Future developers can see what the program
expects and what it rejects.

25
00:01:20.992 --> 00:01:25.346
Reviewers can compare the validation
contract against the later use of the value.

26
00:01:25.426 --> 00:01:27.888
That visibility is especially important in
C,

27
00:01:27.968 --> 00:01:31.060
where a small unchecked assumption can
have wide effects.
